Date: Sun, 14 Feb 93 17:32:36 -0500
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: MEME: Voluntary Social Engineering Through Memetics

Alan Barksdale writes a very interesting posting about engineering religions.
In it he discusses the possible benefits of organizing a religious meme-set
whose purpose is the furthering of certain values such as freedom, peace...
He stresses that a good approach might be to take an existing religion such as
Christianity and apply psychological and advertising techniques to transform it
into something more appropriate for the modern world.  The advantages of this
are that existing believers will support this because they will think it will
help the spread of their religion, and non-believers will see it as a valuable
gift to humanity.  (Please correct me if I mis-paraphrased you, Alan)

I am an atheist, but I do see the value of religions as a set of moral
guidelines.  People will decide what is right and wrong and in the absence of
moral codes many will choose guidelines with devastating social consequences.
Greed and crime have always been around, but in the modern world the waning of
religions have left a huge spiritual vacuum.  This waning was inevitable
(IMHO), caused by the increasingly apparent contradictions between what one
believes and what one experiences.  Scientific progress and cultural mixing
make the old religions simply inappropriate.

I believe this is natural.  It is analogous to the development of a man - at
some time he will be forced to leave the shelter of truth imposed by others
and have to find it on his own (excuse the sexist pronouns).  So we are in a
period of adolescence - a confusing time...

I disagree that we should mold and shape the existing religions to fit the new
world.  At the basis of any such attempt, no matter how clever, would be
hypocrisy.  Any meme-set that is grounded on falsehood will not survive in the
world, for the same reason all the other religions are not surviving (By this I
mean they are not surviving among the technologically advancing society that I
am a part of, and that I expect to be the future).  What is necessary is a kind
of spiritual realism, a belief in the value and meaning in life without the
need of denial of basic facts.  I think that not only is this possible, but that
it is already developing.  A first step might involve a unification of
religious beliefs (as described by Joseph Campbell perhaps) to explore the
similarity in their basic values.  However, ultimately the only beliefs that
will suffice are ones that are self-consistent and honest, and able to change.
Environmentalism? Extropianism? Anarcho-capitalism?  I doubt it will be any of
these, but it's less likely that it will be a religion...

Provided we can conceive of a satisfactory meme-set for the modern world,
should we go about engineering it?  Well, the key question is: do we know what
we are doing?  Alan has made this point.  There could be undesirable side
effects.  There could also be violence - people are often willing to fight for
their beliefs.  Possibly the most serious problem would be one of abuse, those
able to actually affect a society's beliefs will have a tremendous amount of
power, and we know what can happen then.  Safeguards?  Self-organization?
Cross-inhibition of power?  Are we willing to undertake such experimentation?
I suggest it might be too early.  We really know so little about human
psychology and even less about social psychology, that to attempt to shape it
would be like flipping random bits in a computer to see what happens.  Social
development is an adaptive process, we can study it and attempt to fix things
as they seem to go wrong, but trying to guide it is hasty (though an
interesting topic of discussion).

Is anyone aware of any convincing scientific treatment of purposeful social
engineering?  (Are advertising and propaganda satisfactory methods?)

-Paul

