Date: Mon, 15 Feb 93 18:11:41 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: NEUR: silicon neuron

Terry Sejnowski (I think I spelled that right) recently delivered a
talk here, entitled "The Computational Brain" (after his book).  One
interesting tidbit he mentioned was a series of "silicon neuron"
chips being researched at, I think, Caltech.  These differ from the
neural-net-in-hardware chips made by Intel and others, which aim not
at biological fidelity but at running sum-of-products operations
very fast.  The present generation is an analog simulation of the
ion channels in a multi-compartment (i.e. not spatially homogeneous)
neuron.  It matches with some fidelity the action-potential trains
produced by a biological neuron with a given imposed current.  Axonal
transmission could presumably be modeled with a truckload of
compartments and a diffusion term, though this hardly seems
efficient.  Synaptic transmission, I think, still needs some work.

The present simulations have on the order of a dozen compartments, I
believe, and take a few thousand gates.  Not too many full-scale
neurons would fit on a present-generation VLSI, but it beats
numerically cranking the DE's in software.

