Heath Graham Goebel asks:
> I'm interested to know how Perry and other extropians plan 
> to rid themselves of the state. Are you planning on waiting
> until Vernor Vinge's 2030, do you expect the America's socialist
> bent to collapse, the libertarian pary, or do you have another plan?

I see three main elements to the revolution:  reform, vonu and emigration.

Reform means de-legitimizing coercion.  We need to teach the people the
habit of asking, "Do we need a state program for that?"  That's the role I
see for the LP -- to convey this lesson explicitly and in-your-face (Cato
isn't explicit enough imho).

Vonu means living invisible to the state.  I think it's a wholly made-up
word.  "Crypto anarchy" can be a kind of vonu.  My own dream is nomadism in
a solar-powered nanotech "space suit" (with food replicator).  I imagine
each of us hides some things from Big Brother in different little ways.  (I
feel a bit of an ass for updating my voter registration when I moved, but as
a candidate I felt obligated.)

Emigration is obvious -- if settlers on the seas or asteroids are lax about
setting up a state, the respite might be enough to build a complete PPL
order.  (Complete?  Well, I think PPL like science is never complete, but
you know what I mean.)

Dale:
> . . . PPLs could be created in the
> current society by simply writing the needed contracts.  . . .

I want the Constitution to say something like "A judgment by any agency,
public or private, whose jurisdiction was accepted by all parties, shall not
be questioned."  (Here we get into the meta-law of consent, on which Tom's
paper touches.)

