          >vonu. [...]

          "Vonu" is presented as a Polynesian term meaning "immunity
          to coercion", principally, but not necessarily limited to,
          that of the state.  Some of its tenets and techniques, which
          amount to a sort of intensive nomadic reclusiveness, can be
          most easily found in the _Loompanics' Greatest Hits_
          anthology [ca. 1990], which sports several essays by the
          originator of the discipline, "El Rayo".  Loompanics also
          publishes his book[s?].  There also used to be some vonuist
          'zines, although I don't recall seeing any listed in the
          reborn _Factsheet Five_ (They gave a good, if bemused,
          review of _Extropy_ #9, BTW).

          If anyone else has newer or better info on _vonu_, I for
          one would like to hear about it; it strikes me as a
          fascinating nontechnological approach to a lot of the stuff
          we bandy about here, cultivated almost as a martial art
          would be.  Rayo himself is an interesting figure; Bob Black
          refers to him as (approximated quote) "a superbly logical
          thinker who followed his own line of reasoning so rigorously
          that he disappeared completely a few years ago".

          --Blair Haworth


