Date: Tue, 16 Feb 93 11:19:26 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: KIDS: Not raising 'em

Alexander Chislenko posts:

>   Well, donating sperm looks like a very extropian activity...
>Can you tell us more about it?   Do they screen the donors?

I have looked at the donor screening policies of two sperm banks, and they
both required extensive background information.  The banks took special
interest in genetically and sexually transmitted diseases.  I cannot be
sure they disallow donations from those who carry such diseases, since I
passed the tests, but I suspect they would.  They also ask questions about
lifestyle, education, general health, etc.  And only a fraction of would-be
donors have sperm that is robust enought to withstand the necessary
processing.

>Do the recipients know what was the hight/weight/race/IQ/cuteness of
>the donor?  

Yes on all points except the last.  At least where I donated, they did not
take photos of the donors.  Clever donors can figure out ways to inform
recipients about their visual features, however.  You can note if you have
done any modeling, for example.  And because I was friendly with the lab
assistants who worked at the bank, they once said to the male member of a
couple touring the facilities in anticipation of receiving donations, "Hey,
we have a donor [me] who looks just like you!"  They then gave the couple
my donation number.

>Are you paid for being a donor? 

I was paid $75 per good donation.  I could make three acceptable donations
a week in ideal circumstances.  Not all donations, however, even from
someone who has passed all the screening processes, make the grade.

>Does the amount depend on your IQ?  

Not as far as I know.  A high IQ will help to guarantee that your sperm
finds a home, though, as parents tend to like that sort of thing.

>   Do you have any idea of how many kids may carry your genes?

I can only guess that it is somewhere between 0 and 50.  I would have to
know the ratio between sperm samples received and children carried to term
to get a more accurate estimate.  From contacting the bank after I had left
the program, I do know that most if not all of my many donations were sold
to would-be parents.

>   Do you have an 800 number one might call to help improve the global
>genetic pool?

No, though many major university student newspapers carry ads for sperm
banks.  Students are the favored source of donations--they are young,
reasonably smart, geographically concentrated, and need cash.  I personally
know of sperm banks in LA, CA (in Westwood, near UCLA); Berkeley, CA (near
UCB); and in Vienna, VA.

Be forewarned, though:  donorship will demand that you reveal a lot about
your past.  It can also put a severe crimp in your sex life, as you have to
wait two to three days between ejaculations to build up sufficient sperm
for an acceptable donation.  Some folks also find the whole process too
unnatural to stomach.


